<% content_for :title, "Resources" %>
<% content_for :head do %>
  <%= stylesheet_link_tag "analysis-icons", "resources", "data-turbo-track": "reload" %>
<% end %>

<div class="resources">
  <%= render "layouts/header" %>
  <main class="resources-main">
    <section class="resources-intro" aria-labelledby="resources-heading">
      <h1 id="resources-heading">MicroNet: Background</h1>
      <p>
        MicroNet builds on the work of Peter Tonner and Firas Midani. It uses AMIGA, the Python
        microbial growth analysis pipeline developed by Firas and based in part on Peter’s work.
        For an in-depth explanation of the pipeline, see Firas’s
        <a href="https://firasmidani.github.io/amiga/">AMIGA vignette</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        MicroNet makes these methods available through a web interface for researchers who want
        to visualize growth curves and perform growth analyses without command-line tools.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="resources-demo" aria-labelledby="demo-heading">
      <h2 id="demo-heading">Demo Video</h2>
      <div class="resources-video-container">
        <video controls preload="metadata" aria-label="MicroNet demonstration">
          <source src="/docs/MicroNet Demo.mp4" type="video/mp4">
          Your browser does not support the video tag.
        </video>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="resources-analyses" aria-labelledby="analyses-heading">
      <h2 id="analyses-heading" class="section-heading">Specific Analyses</h2>
      <div class="resources-analysis-grid">
        <article id="raw-data" class="resource-analysis" aria-labelledby="raw-data-heading">
          <div class="resource-analysis-heading">
            <span class="analysis-icon icon-bars" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            <h3 id="raw-data-heading">Raw Data Plot</h3>
          </div>
          <p>
            This analysis generates a growth curve for each well in your dataset and reports fold change
            relative to the negative-control well, as described in the
            <a href="https://firasmidani.github.io/amiga/doc/plotting.html">AMIGA vignette</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            Fold change is the maximum change in optical density (OD) from the first time point in a well,
            divided by the corresponding maximum change in the negative-control well. The numbers in the
            upper-right corner of each plot show the raw maximum OD for that curve. Wells with a fold change
            above 1.5 are highlighted in blue; those below 0.5 are highlighted in red.
          </p>
          <p class="resource-requirement">
            Each uploaded data file must contain exactly 96 wells (a standard microplate).
            Files with a different number of wells are rejected for this analysis.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article id="gaussian-process-plot" class="resource-analysis" aria-labelledby="gp-heading">
          <div class="resource-analysis-heading">
            <span class="analysis-icon icon-curve" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            <h3 id="gp-heading">Gaussian Process Plot</h3>
          </div>
          <p>
            This analysis fits a Gaussian Process regression to your growth curves and plots the resulting
            model of normalized OD over time. Smoothing measurement noise helps reveal the underlying growth
            trend. The thin lines surrounding each curve show 95% confidence intervals.
          </p>
          <p>
            Subset filters select the conditions included in the plot. You can filter on multiple metadata
            columns: a well can match any selected value within a filter, but must match every filter to be
            included.
          </p>
          <p>
            You can also pool replicates by one or more metadata columns. Wells with the same combination of
            values in those columns are modeled together, so each curve represents a pooled condition.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article id="fit-statistics" class="resource-analysis" aria-labelledby="fit-heading">
          <div class="resource-analysis-heading">
            <span class="analysis-icon icon-stats" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            <h3 id="fit-heading">Fit Statistics</h3>
          </div>
          <p>
            This analysis uses AMIGA’s <code>fit</code> function to extract quantitative growth parameters
            from the Gaussian Process fit of each sample. MicroNet reports the area under the curve (AUC,
            in units of OD), growth rate, linear carrying capacity (in units of OD), lag time, and doubling time.
          </p>
          <p>
            Without pooling, statistics are reported per well. If you pool replicates by one or more metadata
            columns, the statistics are reported for each pooled condition instead.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article id="heatmap" class="resource-analysis" aria-labelledby="heatmap-heading">
          <div class="resource-analysis-heading">
            <span class="analysis-icon icon-grid" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            <h3 id="heatmap-heading">Heatmap Plot</h3>
          </div>
          <p>
            This analysis displays a single growth parameter across two metadata dimensions as a
            color-coded grid. Choose the parameter to display (for example, growth rate) and the metadata
            columns for the x- and y-axes. MicroNet runs an AMIGA fit to obtain the parameter values, then
            renders the heatmap.
          </p>
          <p>
            Use this view to spot patterns across a plate layout or across combinations of conditions.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article id="test-differential-growth" class="resource-analysis" aria-labelledby="test-heading">
          <div class="resource-analysis-heading">
            <span class="analysis-icon icon-hypothesis" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            <h3 id="test-heading">Test Hypothesis</h3>
          </div>
          <p>
            This analysis uses Gaussian Process regression and AMIGA’s <code>test</code> function to test
            for differential growth between two conditions. It uses AMIGA’s default of 99 permutations.
            See the <a href="https://firasmidani.github.io/amiga/doc/hypothesistesting.html">AMIGA
            hypothesis-testing guide</a> for details of the method and its command-line usage.
          </p>
          <p>
            The output includes a normalized OD plot modeling the growth of the two selected conditions,
            with thin lines showing 95% confidence intervals. The report gives the log Bayes Factor and
            the confidence interval it must fall outside to indicate differential growth at a false
            discovery rate (FDR) of 10%.
          </p>
          <p>
            The time step size affects this analysis’s memory use and temporal resolution. See
            <a href="#time-step-size">Time Step Size and Memory</a> for the available settings, minimum
            number of time points, and automatic retry behavior.
          </p>
          <p>
            For help interpreting the output, <a href="/contact">contact us</a> and someone on our team
            can assist you by email.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article id="compare-parameters" class="resource-analysis" aria-labelledby="compare-heading">
          <div class="resource-analysis-heading">
            <span class="analysis-icon icon-compare" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            <h3 id="compare-heading">Compare Parameters</h3>
          </div>
          <p>
            This analysis uses AMIGA’s <code>compare</code> function to statistically compare growth
            parameters between pooled conditions. It fits the data while sampling from the posterior
            distribution, providing both point estimates of each growth parameter and a measure of
            uncertainty around the difference between conditions.
          </p>
          <p>
            Use this for a quantitative comparison of growth parameter values. It complements the
            visual comparison of growth curves provided by the Gaussian Process Plot.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="time-step-size" class="resources-method-note" aria-labelledby="time-step-heading">
      <h2 id="time-step-heading">Time Step Size and Memory</h2>
      <p>
        Analyses that fit Gaussian Process models—the Gaussian Process Plot, Fit Statistics,
        Heatmap, Compare Parameters, and Test Hypothesis—use a <em>time step size</em> that you
        choose during upload. The default is <%= Job::DEFAULT_TSS %>; you can choose values
        from <%= Job::DEFAULT_TSS %> to <%= Job::USER_SELECTABLE_TSS_MAX %>.
      </p>
      <p>
        A time step size of 1 keeps every measurement. Larger values fit the model on fewer
        time points, reducing memory use at the cost of temporal resolution. This can help with
        large datasets, but may miss rapid changes in growth. MicroNet checks your choice against
        the uploaded data so at least <%= Job::MIN_GP_TIME_POINTS %> time points remain for fitting;
        if too few would remain, you must choose a smaller value.
      </p>
      <p>
        If fitting runs out of memory, MicroNet automatically retries with progressively larger
        time step sizes. It makes up to <%= Job::MAX_TSS_ATTEMPTS - 1 %> additional attempts,
        increasing the step size by <%= Job::TSS_RETRY_INCREMENT %> each time, provided at least
        <%= Job::MIN_GP_TIME_POINTS %> time points remain. If all permitted attempts fail, the
        analysis is reported as failed.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>
  <%= render "layouts/footer" %>
</div>
